**1. Analysis of the Attached PDF (2015 Journal of Controlled Release and 2017 npj Vaccines):**

The PDFs describe Nano-11, a dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle, as an effective and safe vaccine adjuvant. Nano-11, with a positive surface charge, enhances the uptake of adsorbed antigens by dendritic cells (DCs) and activates them in vitro, leading to increased expression of costimulatory molecules and IL-1β/IL-12p40 secretion. *In vivo*, Nano-11 formulations significantly boost antigen-specific antibody responses, comparable to or exceeding aluminum hydroxide adjuvant. It induces a transient, localized inflammatory response dominated by macrophages/monocytes, clears gradually from the injection site, and efficiently targets antigens to migratory DCs in draining lymph nodes.

**2. Independent Analysis of the Image:**

The image displays flow cytometry data from mice injected with Nano-11 across Day 1, 2, and 7. Dot plots track Nano-11 alongside CD11c and MHCII expression in myeloid cells from draining lymph nodes. Bar charts quantify Nano-11-containing cell percentages over time, distribution among Migratory Dendritic Cells (MigDC), Lymphoid Tissue Resident Dendritic Cells (LTDC), and Monocytes/Macrophages (Mo/MΦ), and Nano-11 mean fluorescence intensity (MFI) within these cell types. Nano-11-positive cells peak at Day 2, with MigDCs being the most prevalent Nano-11-containing population and showing the highest Nano-11 MFI per cell.

